# Unity ECS Patterns for Mass AI

Production patterns for Unity's Data-Oriented Technology Stack (DOTS) specifically tailored for large-scale AI simulations, including zombie hordes, flocking, and crowd behaviors.

## When to Use This Skill

- Simulating thousands of autonomous agents
- Implementing simple movement and health systems with ECS
- Managing entity lifecycles for large populations
- Leveraging Burst-compiled jobs for performance
- Spawning and despawning entities efficiently

## Core Concepts

### 1. ECS for AI

| Aspect | Traditional OOP | ECS/DOTS |
|--------|-----------------|----------|
| Data layout | Object-oriented | Data-oriented |
| Memory | Scattered | Contiguous |
| Processing | Per-object | Batched |
| Scaling | Poor with count | Linear scaling |
| Best for | Complex behaviors | Mass simulation with simple rules |

### 2. DOTS Components for AI

```
Entity: Lightweight ID (no data)
Component: Pure data (no behavior)
System: Logic that processes components
World: Container for entities
Archetype: Unique combination of components
Chunk: Memory block for same-archetype entities
```

## Patterns

### Pattern 1: Basic AI Components

```csharp
using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.Mathematics;

// Movement data
public struct Movement : IComponentData
{
    public float Speed;
    public float3 Direction; // Updated each frame
}

// Health data
public struct Health : IComponentData
{
    public float Current;
    public float Max;
}

// Waypoint follower (simple list of positions)
[InternalBufferCapacity(4)]
public struct Waypoint : IBufferElementData
{
    public float3 Position;
}

// Tag component for AI entities
public struct AITag : IComponentData { }
public struct DeadTag : IComponentData { }
```

### Pattern 2: Simple Movement System (No Pathfinding)

```csharp
using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.Transforms;
using Unity.Mathematics;
using Unity.Burst;

[BurstCompile]
public partial struct MoveAIForwardSystem : ISystem
{
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        float deltaTime = SystemAPI.Time.DeltaTime;

        foreach (var (transform, movement) in
            SystemAPI.Query<RefRW<LocalTransform>, RefRO<Movement>>())
        {
            // Simple movement in a fixed direction – no obstacle avoidance
            transform.ValueRW.Position += movement.ValueRO.Direction * movement.ValueRO.Speed * deltaTime;
        }
    }
}
```

### Pattern 3: Health and Death System

```csharp
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct HealthSystem : ISystem
{
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        // Mark entities with zero health as dead
        foreach (var (health, entity) in
            SystemAPI.Query<RefRW<Health>>()
                .WithNone<DeadTag>()
                .WithEntityAccess())
        {
            if (health.ValueRO.Current <= 0)
            {
                // Add dead tag – structural change deferred via ECB (see Pattern 5)
                SystemAPI.GetSingleton<EndSimulationEntityCommandBufferSystem.Singleton>()
                    .CreateCommandBuffer(state.WorldUnmanaged)
                    .AddComponent<DeadTag>(entity);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Pattern 4: Waypoint Following (Basic)

```csharp
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct WaypointSystem : ISystem
{
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        foreach (var (transform, waypoints, movement) in
            SystemAPI.Query<RefRW<LocalTransform>, DynamicBuffer<Waypoint>, RefRW<Movement>>())
        {
            if (waypoints.Length == 0)
                continue;

            // Move toward the first waypoint
            float3 target = waypoints[0].Position;
            float3 dir = math.normalize(target - transform.ValueRO.Position);
            movement.ValueRW.Direction = dir;

            // If close enough, remove that waypoint
            if (math.distance(transform.ValueRO.Position, target) < 0.5f)
            {
                waypoints.RemoveAt(0);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Pattern 5: Spawning and Despawning with Command Buffers

```csharp
using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.Transforms;

[BurstCompile]
[UpdateInGroup(typeof(SimulationSystemGroup))]
public partial struct SpawnDespawnSystem : ISystem
{
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        var ecbSingleton = SystemAPI.GetSingleton<BeginSimulationEntityCommandBufferSystem.Singleton>();
        var ecb = ecbSingleton.CreateCommandBuffer(state.WorldUnmanaged);

        // Spawn new zombie when a spawner component exists
        foreach (var (spawner, transform) in
            SystemAPI.Query<RefRW<Spawner>, RefRO<LocalTransform>>())
        {
            spawner.ValueRW.Timer -= SystemAPI.Time.DeltaTime;
            if (spawner.ValueRO.Timer <= 0)
            {
                spawner.ValueRW.Timer = spawner.ValueRO.Interval;

                // Instantiate prefab entity
                Entity newEntity = ecb.Instantiate(spawner.ValueRO.Prefab);

                // Set initial transform
                ecb.SetComponent(newEntity, new LocalTransform
                {
                    Position = transform.ValueRO.Position,
                    Rotation = quaternion.identity,
                    Scale = 1f
                });

                // Add a random initial direction
                ecb.AddComponent(newEntity, new Movement
                {
                    Speed = 5f,
                    Direction = new float3(0, 0, 1) // all move forward
                });
                ecb.AddComponent(newEntity, new Health { Current = 100, Max = 100 });
                ecb.AddComponent(newEntity, new AITag());
            }
        }

        // Despawn dead zombies
        foreach (var (deadTag, entity) in
            SystemAPI.Query<RefRO<DeadTag>>().WithEntityAccess())
        {
            ecb.DestroyEntity(entity);
        }
    }
}
```

