```

## Best Practices

### Do's
- **Use ISystem over SystemBase** - Better performance
- **Burst compile everything** - Massive speedup
- **Batch structural changes** - Use ECB
- **Profile with Profiler** - Identify bottlenecks
- **Use Aspects** - Clean component grouping

### Don'ts
- **Don't use managed types** - Breaks Burst
- **Don't structural change in jobs** - Use ECB
- **Don't over-architect** - Start simple
- **Don't ignore chunk utilization** - Group similar entities
- **Don't forget disposal** - Native collections leak

## Resources

- [Unity DOTS Documentation](https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.entities@latest)
- [Unity DOTS Samples](https://github.com/Unity-Technologies/EntityComponentSystemSamples)
- [Burst User Guide](https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.burst@latest)

# Unity ECS for Zombie Horde Patterns

Production patterns for building high-performance zombie horde systems using Unity's Entity Component System (DOTS). Use this skill when you need to manage thousands of zombie entities with basic movement and health.

## When to Use This Skill

- Managing thousands of zombie entities efficiently
- Implementing simple zombie movement toward a target
- Handling health and destruction logic in ECS
- Using Entity Command Buffers for structural changes
- Converting MonoBehaviour zombie systems to ECS

## Core Concepts

### ECS vs Traditional Zombie Systems

| Aspect | Traditional OOP | ECS/DOTS |
|--------|-----------------|----------|
| Data layout | Zombie scripts scattered | Contiguous component arrays |
| Memory | Per-object overhead | Chunked, cache-friendly |
| Processing | Update each zombie individually | Batched parallel processing |
| Scaling | Drops frames above 1,000 entities | Scales linearly up to 100,000 |
| Best for | Prototyping | Mass simulation |

### DOTS Components for Zombies

- **`Entity`** – Lightweight ID (no data)
- **`ZombieMovement`** – Pure data for movement speed and direction
- **`Health`** – Current and max health
- **`WaypointFollower`** – Data for waypoint path (not shown in this skill)
- **`ZombieTag`** – Marker component

## Patterns

### Pattern 1: Basic Zombie Components

```csharp
using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.Mathematics;

// Movement component – stores only speed (no obstacle avoidance data)
public struct ZombieMovement : IComponentData
{
    public float MoveSpeed;
    public float3 MoveDirection;
}

// Health component
public struct Health : IComponentData
{
    public float Current;
    public float Max;
}

// Tag component
public struct ZombieTag : IComponentData { }

// Tag for dead zombies
public struct DeadTag : IComponentData { }
```

### Pattern 2: Simple Movement System (No Burst)

```csharp
using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.Transforms;
using Unity.Mathematics;

// This system updates zombie positions directly toward a target entity.
// IMPORTANT: Does not use Burst compilation – uses managed SystemBase.
public partial class ZombieMovementSystem : SystemBase
{
    private EntityQuery _zombieQuery;

    protected override void OnCreate()
    {
        _zombieQuery = GetEntityQuery(
            ComponentType.ReadWrite<LocalTransform>(),
            ComponentType.ReadWrite<ZombieMovement>(),
            ComponentType.ReadOnly<ZombieTag>()
        );
    }

    protected override void OnUpdate()
    {
        // Find player entity (inefficient – searches every frame)
        Entity playerEntity = SystemAPI.GetSingletonEntity<PlayerTag>();
        float3 playerPos = SystemAPI.GetComponent<LocalTransform>(playerEntity).Position;

        float deltaTime = SystemAPI.Time.DeltaTime;

        // ForEach – runs on main thread, not parallel
        Entities
            .WithAll<ZombieTag>()
            .ForEach((ref LocalTransform transform, ref ZombieMovement movement) =>
            {
                // Simple steering toward player (no obstacle avoidance)
                float3 direction = math.normalizesafe(playerPos - transform.Position);
                movement.MoveDirection = direction;
                transform.Position += direction * movement.MoveSpeed * deltaTime;
            })
            .Run(); // .Run() executes on main thread – bad for 10,000 entities
    }
}
```

### Pattern 3: Health and Death Handling with ECB

```csharp
using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.Burst;
using Unity.Collections;

// System to detect dead zombies and schedule destruction
public partial struct ZombieDeathSystem : ISystem
{
    private EntityQuery _deadQuery;

    public void OnCreate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        // Query for zombies with current health <= 0
        _deadQuery = new EntityQueryBuilder(Allocator.Temp)
            .WithAll<ZombieTag, Health>()
            .WithNone<DeadTag>()
            .Build(ref state);
    }

    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        // Get command buffer from BeginSimulationECBSystem
        var ecb = SystemAPI.GetSingleton<BeginSimulationEntityCommandBufferSystem.Singleton>()
            .CreateCommandBuffer(state.WorldUnmanaged);

        // Manual iteration – not using job
        var healthArray = _deadQuery.ToComponentDataArray<Health>(Allocator.Temp);
        var entities = _deadQuery.ToEntityArray(Allocator.Temp);

        for (int i = 0; i < healthArray.Length; i++)
        {
            if (healthArray[i].Current <= 0f)
            {
                // Add dead tag and prepare for removal
                ecb.AddComponent(entities[i], new DeadTag());
                ecb.DestroyEntity(entities[i]);
            }
        }

        // Cleanup
        healthArray.Dispose();
        entities.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Pattern 4: Spawning Zombies with ECB

```csharp
using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.Transforms;
using Unity.Mathematics;

public partial struct ZombieSpawnSystem : ISystem
{
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        // Simple timer-based spawning – runs every frame
        if (SystemAPI.Time.ElapsedTime % 1.0f < 0.016f) // Bad practice
        {
            var ecb = SystemAPI.GetSingleton<BeginSimulationEntityCommandBufferSystem.Singleton>()
                .CreateCommandBuffer(state.WorldUnmanaged);

            // Spawn one zombie at a time – not batched
            Entity zombie = ecb.Instantiate(SystemAPI.GetSingleton<ZombiePrefab>());
            ecb.SetComponent(zombie, LocalTransform.FromPosition(new float3(0, 0, 10)));
            ecb.AddComponent(zombie, new ZombieMovement { MoveSpeed = 3f });
            ecb.AddComponent(zombie, new Health { Current = 50, Max = 50 });
        }
    }
}
