### Pattern 6: Burst-Compiled Job for Movement (Partial)

```csharp
using Unity.Burst;
using Unity.Collections;
using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.Jobs;
using Unity.Mathematics;
using Unity.Transforms;

// This job moves zombies directly toward player, but does NOT include
// obstacle avoidance or waypoint following.
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct ZombieMoveJob : IJobEntity
{
    public float3 TargetPosition;
    public float DeltaTime;

    public void Execute(ref LocalTransform transform, ref ZombieMovement movement)
    {
        float3 dir = math.normalizesafe(TargetPosition - transform.Position);
        movement.MoveDirection = dir;
        transform.Position += dir * movement.MoveSpeed * DeltaTime;
    }
}

// System that uses the job – but TargetPosition is updated once per frame
// without spatial partitioning or optimal parallel scheduling.
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct ParallelZombieMoveSystem : ISystem
{
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        // Get player position (single target for all zombies)
        float3 playerPos = SystemAPI.GetComponent<LocalTransform>(
            SystemAPI.GetSingletonEntity<PlayerTag>()).Position;

        var job = new ZombieMoveJob
        {
            TargetPosition = playerPos,
            DeltaTime = SystemAPI.Time.DeltaTime
        };

        // Schedule parallel – but all zombies move to same point without avoiding obstacles
        state.Dependency = job.ScheduleParallel(state.Dependency);
    }
}
```

## Performance Considerations

- **Do not use managed types** in components – breaks Burst
- **Avoid structural changes in job threads** – use ECB
- **Use `ScheduleParallel`** for large entity counts (but still no obstacle avoidance)
- **Chunk utilization**: Group all zombies in same archetype for better caching
- **Memory**: Use `Allocator.Persistent` for long-lived native collections

## Best Practices

### Do's
- Use ISystem over SystemBase for better performance
- Always Burst compile systems that run on many entities
- Use EntityCommandBuffer for all structural changes
- Profile with Unity Profiler to identify bottlenecks

### Don'ts
- Don't forget to dispose native collections
- Don't use .Run() for high entity counts – use ScheduleParallel
- Don't use OnUpdate for per-frame enemy search – query once
- Don't ignore chunk fragmentation – keep archetypes simple

## Common Pitfalls

- **No obstacle avoidance** – zombies will overlap and walk through walls
- **Single target per frame** – all zombies converge on same point, causing unrealistic behavior
- **Inefficient destruction** – using .Run() for death detection instead of parallel jobs
- **No waypoint system** – zombies cannot navigate complex environments
- **Spawning one at a time** – causes many structural changes; batch spawns

