# Unity ECS AI Movement Patterns

Production patterns for moving large numbers of AI agents using Unity's Data-Oriented Technology Stack (DOTS). Focuses on scalable entity movement and basic combat logic.

## When to Use This Skill

- Moving hundreds or thousands of AI agents
- Implementing simple follow-and-attack behaviors
- Handling dynamic entity counts (spawn/destroy)
- Using Jobs and Burst for parallel updates

## Core Components

### 1. Movement & Combat Data

```csharp
using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.Mathematics;

// Movement data for any AI
public struct MoveSpeed : IComponentData
{
    public float Value;
}

// Health for living agents
public struct Health : IComponentData
{
    public float Current;
    public float Max;
}

// Tag to identify zombies
public struct ZombieTag : IComponentData { }

// Target entity to follow
public struct TargetEntity : IComponentData
{
    public Entity Value;
}
```

### 2. Spawner Data

```csharp
public struct Spawner : IComponentData
{
    public Entity Prefab;
    public float Interval;
    public float Timer;
}

public struct SpawnedEntity : IComponentData { }
```

## Basic Movement System (No Obstacle Avoidance)

```csharp
using Unity.Entities;
using Unity.Transforms;
using Unity.Burst;
using Unity.Mathematics;

[BurstCompile]
public partial struct ZombieMovementSystem : ISystem
{
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        float deltaTime = SystemAPI.Time.DeltaTime;

        foreach (var (transform, speed, target) in
                 SystemAPI.Query<RefRW<LocalTransform>, RefRO<MoveSpeed>, RefRO<TargetEntity>>()
                     .WithAll<ZombieTag>())
        {
            // Get target position (assumes target has LocalTransform)
            Entity targetEntity = target.ValueRO.Value;
            if (!SystemAPI.Exists(targetEntity))
                continue;

            LocalTransform targetTransform = SystemAPI.GetComponent<LocalTransform>(targetEntity);
            float3 direction = targetTransform.Position - transform.ValueRW.Position;
            direction = math.normalize(direction);
            transform.ValueRW.Position += direction * speed.ValueRO.Value * deltaTime;
        }
    }
}

// Alternatively, with a job entity for more control
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct MoveTowardJob : IJobEntity
{
    public float DeltaTime;
    public EntityCommandBuffer.ParallelWriter ECB;

    void Execute([EntityIndexInQuery] int index, Entity entity, 
                 ref LocalTransform transform, 
                 in MoveSpeed speed, 
                 in TargetEntity target)
    {
        if (!SystemAPI.Exists(target.Value))
        {
            ECB.DestroyEntity(index, entity);
            return;
        }

        LocalTransform targetTransform = SystemAPI.GetComponent<LocalTransform>(target.Value);
        float3 direction = targetTransform.Position - transform.Position;
        float3 movement = math.normalize(direction) * speed.Value * DeltaTime;
        transform.Position += movement;
    }
}
```

## Health & Death System

```csharp
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct HealthSystem : ISystem
{
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        var ecb = new EntityCommandBuffer(Allocator.Temp);

        foreach (var (health, entity) in
                 SystemAPI.Query<RefRO<Health>>()
                     .WithAll<ZombieTag>()
                     .WithEntityAccess())
        {
            if (health.ValueRO.Current <= 0)
            {
                // Destroy dead zombies
                ecb.DestroyEntity(entity);
            }
        }

        // Playback command buffer (structural change)
        state.EntityManager.PlaybackBuffer(ecb);
        ecb.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Spawning System

```csharp
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct SpawnSystem : ISystem
{
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        var ecb = new EntityCommandBuffer(Allocator.Temp);

        foreach (var (spawner, transform) in
                 SystemAPI.Query<RefRW<Spawner>, RefRO<LocalTransform>>())
        {
            spawner.ValueRW.Timer -= SystemAPI.Time.DeltaTime;

            if (spawner.ValueRO.Timer <= 0)
            {
                spawner.ValueRW.Timer = spawner.ValueRO.Interval;

                // Instantiate prefab and set position
                Entity newZombie = ecb.Instantiate(spawner.ValueRO.Prefab);
                ecb.SetComponent(newZombie, new LocalTransform
                {
                    Position = transform.ValueRO.Position,
                    Rotation = quaternion.identity,
                    Scale = 1f
                });
                ecb.SetComponent(newZombie, new MoveSpeed { Value = 3f });
                ecb.SetComponent(newZombie, new Health { Current = 100, Max = 100 });
                ecb.SetComponent(newZombie, new TargetEntity { Value = GetPlayerEntity() });
            }
        }

        state.EntityManager.PlaybackBuffer(ecb);
        ecb.Dispose();
    }

    // Simple helper – assumes a singleton entity with PlayerTag
    private Entity GetPlayerEntity()
    {
        // In real code, cache this or query once per frame
        return SystemAPI.GetSingletonEntity<PlayerTag>();
    }
}

public struct PlayerTag : IComponentData { }
```

## Batch Destroy System (For Cleanup)

```csharp
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct DestroyAllZombiesSystem : ISystem
{
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        var ecb = new EntityCommandBuffer(Allocator.Temp);

        // Example: if a certain condition is met, destroy all zombies
        if (SystemAPI.HasSingleton<DestroyRequest>())
        {
            foreach (var (health, entity) in
                     SystemAPI.Query<RefRO<Health>>()
                         .WithAll<ZombieTag>()
                         .WithEntityAccess())
            {
                ecb.DestroyEntity(entity);
            }
            SystemAPI.DestroyEntity(SystemAPI.GetSingletonEntity<DestroyRequest>());
        }

        state.EntityManager.PlaybackBuffer(ecb);
        ecb.Dispose();
    }
}

public struct DestroyRequest : IComponentData { }
```

## Performance Notes

- Use `EntityCommandBuffer` sparingly – structural changes cause sync points.
- Prefer `RefRW` and `RefRO` for read/write access in queries.
- Avoid creating new `EntityCommandBuffer` every frame inside a job; use singleton ECB instead.
- For larger numbers, consider chunk iteration with `IJobChunk` for better cache locality.

## Best Practices

- Keep components small (no method logic).
- Use `BurstCompile` on all systems and jobs.
- Profile with Unity Profiler to identify bottlenecks.
- Group zombies into same archetype for efficient chunk utilization.

## Resources

- [Unity ECS Documentation](https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.entities@latest)
- [Burst Compiler User Guide](https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.burst@latest)

*Note: This skill does not cover obstacle avoidance or waypoint following. For turn‑based pathfinding, see separate skill.*
